From ce01432cbd1844967c55d9ef35ba4a8789108cce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Homies Dev <dev@homies.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 06:29:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] feat: accessible password visibility toggle, shared
 across all three forms
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three login/registration forms handled password visibility three
different ways: Login had no toggle at all, Register had one built from a
clickable <span>, and HostelLogin had a single shared flag driving three
separate fields.

The <span> implementation is the problem worth naming. A span with an
onClick is not focusable, not reachable by keyboard, and announces
nothing to a screen reader, so a user who cannot use a mouse had no way
to check what they had typed. HostelLogin's shared flag was worse in a
different way: revealing the login password also revealed the new
password and its confirmation, so an admin registering a hostel on a
projected screen could expose a credential they had no intention of
showing.

Replaced by one PasswordField component. Details that matter:

- The toggle is <button type="button">. A bare <button> inside a <form>
  defaults to type="submit", so tapping the eye control would have
  submitted a half-typed password and returned "Invalid credentials" —
  a confusing failure that looks like a wrong password.
- aria-pressed plus a state-dependent aria-label, so a screen reader user
  is told whether their password is currently visible. Being in that
  state without knowing is the actual risk.
- Each field owns its own visibility state.
- The browser's native reveal control is suppressed, so Edge and Safari
  do not render two of them stacked.

Register also gains live validation: a character-count hint below a
too-short password and a match/mismatch indicator under the confirmation
field, with submit disabled until the form can succeed. Previously a
mismatch was only reported on submit, as a red toast, after the whole
form had been filled in — the most common registration failure, delivered
at the least useful moment.

Rejected: an eye glyph instead of the words Show/Hide. The glyph is
ambiguous about which state it represents (does the open eye mean the
password is visible, or that clicking will make it visible?) and the two
words are unambiguous in both directions.
